    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Sukarno led the nationalist movement against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> rule, spent over a decade in Dutch detention, and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-hatta">Mohammad Hatta</a> declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesian-independence">Indonesian independence</a> on 17 August 1945, after which he led the resistance to Dutch re-colonisation until recognition in 1949.</li><li>In 1959 he replaced a quarrelling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliamentary-democracy">parliamentary system</a> with the authoritarian Guided Democracy, and in the early 1960s he pursued an aggressive foreign policy while the economy deteriorated and the Communist Party grew under his protection.</li><li>After the 30 September Movement of 1965, General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suharto">Suharto</a> took control, between 500,000 and over 1 million people were killed in the purge that followed, and Sukarno spent his last years under house arrest.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In his speech of 1 June 1945 Sukarno set out five principles, known as pancasila, that he saw as shared by all Indonesians; the version put into effect in the 1945 Constitution omitted the reference to Islamic law that an earlier draft had carried.</p></aside>
    </section>

