    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1901</span><span class="rf-text">Sukarno is born in Surabaya, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1927</span><span class="rf-text">He and friends from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandung">Bandung</a> study club found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesian-national-party">Indonesian National Party</a>, and he becomes its first leader</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1930</span><span class="rf-text">At his trial in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandung">Bandung</a> he delivers the speeches published as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> Accuses and is sentenced to four years in prison</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1934</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> governor-general sends him without trial into internal exile, first on Flores and from 1938 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengkulu">Bengkulu</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1945</span><span class="rf-text">Sukarno and Hatta proclaim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesian-independence">Indonesian independence</a> on 17 August, and the next day Sukarno is elected president</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1955</span><span class="rf-text">He hosts the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandung-conference">Bandung Conference</a> of Asian and African countries</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1959</span><span class="rf-text">He restores the 1945 constitution by decree and begins Guided Democracy</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1966</span><span class="rf-text">Under the order known as Supersemar he transfers most presidential authority to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suharto">Suharto</a>, and he is placed under house arrest the next year</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sukarno was born on 6 June 1901 in Surabaya, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a>. His father, Raden Soekemi Sosrodihardjo, was a Muslim Javanese primary school teacher and priyayi from Grobogan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-java">Central Java</a>; his mother, Ida Ayu Nyoman Rai, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> Balinese of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> from Buleleng. He was originally named Kusno Sosrodihardjo and, following Javanese custom, was renamed after surviving a childhood illness. His name comes from Karna, a hero of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>. Like many Javanese, he had only one name.</p><p>After a native primary school he attended a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> primary school in Mojokerto, and in 1916 a Dutch-type secondary school in Surabaya, where he met Tjokroaminoto, a nationalist and founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarekat-islam">Sarekat Islam</a>. In 1920 he married Tjokroaminoto's daughter Siti Oetari. In 1921 he began to study civil engineering, with a focus on architecture, at the Technische Hoogeschool in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandung">Bandung</a> and received an engineer's degree in 1926. He was fluent in several languages, among them Javanese, Sundanese, Balinese, Indonesian and Dutch. As a student he came to despise both traditional Javanese feudalism, which he considered backward and to blame for the country's fall under Dutch rule, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a> of Western countries, which he termed the exploitation of humans by other humans. He blamed it for the poverty and low levels of education of Indonesians.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sutan-sjahrir"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sutan-sjahrir.jpg" alt="Sutan Sjahrir" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sutan-sjahrir">Sutan Sjahrir</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sutan_Sjahrir,_10_Orang_Indonesia_Terbesar_Sekarang_(1952),_p136.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Written by St. Rais Alamsjah, published by Mutiara, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Living under Tjokroaminoto, Sukarno was first exposed to nationalist ideas. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandung">Bandung</a> he read European, American, nationalist, communist and religious political philosophy and developed what he styled Marhaenism, named after a peasant he met who owned and worked his own small plot. He organised a study club, the Algemeene Studieclub, against the student clubs dominated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> students. On 4 July 1927 he and friends from the club founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesian-national-party">Indonesian National Party</a> (PNI), and he was elected its first leader. It advocated independence, secularism and unity among the ethnic groups of the Indies, and opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a>. After the failed communist rebellion of 1926 the party drew many university-educated young people.</p><p>Dutch agents infiltrated his meetings, and he was arrested on 29 December 1929. At his trial in Bandung in 1930 he made long speeches attacking colonialism, titled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> Menggoegat (Indonesia Accuses). He was sentenced to four years in Sukamiskin prison, but press coverage and pressure from liberals in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and the Indies led the governor-general to shorten the term, and he was released on 31 December 1931 as a popular hero. The PNI had split in his absence, and in 1932 he became head of Partindo, which favoured immediate mass agitation, against Hatta's and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutan-sjahrir">Sutan Sjahrir</a>'s slower strategy of educating a leadership. He was arrested again on 1 August 1933, and the governor-general used emergency powers to send him into internal exile without trial, to Ende on Flores in 1934 and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengkulu">Bengkulu</a> in 1938.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Japanese invaded the Indies in 1942 and released the nationalist leaders. Sukarno chose to cooperate, wanting to use the Japanese to gain independence, and he headed mass organisations the Japanese set up that promoted recruitment of romusha, the forced labourers sent to build railways and airfields, and the requisitioning of food. Food requisitioning caused a famine on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> that killed more than one million people in 1944 and 1945; in Sukarno's view these were necessary sacrifices for future independence, and in later years he was lastingly ashamed of his part in the romusha. On 1 June 1945 he presented pancasila to the committee preparing independence: nationalism, internationalism, democracy, social justice and belief in God. On 17 August 1945, after youth leaders had pressed him to act on the Japanese surrender, he and Hatta proclaimed the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, and the next day Sukarno was elected president.</p><p>He then sought recognition abroad rather than battle with British and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> forces. Two Dutch military offensives, in July 1947 and December 1948, were followed by international pressure, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> transferred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> on 27 December 1949. As president he organised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandung-conference">Bandung Conference</a> in 1955 and, in 1961, helped found the Non-Aligned Movement at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>. In 1959 he reinstated the 1945 constitution by decree, and from 1960 he built his government on Nasakom, a union of nationalism, religion and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>. He had a hand in designing monuments and the Gelora Bung Karno sports complex.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Sukarno&#8221; cites where it covers Investigating Committee for Preparatory Work for Independence, Foreign policy, Foreign honours:</p><ul><li>Derek A. Ide, Ghosts of Bandung: Black Power and Palestine in the Global Cold War (University of California Press, 2026)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The parliamentary years from 1950 were unstable. Darul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> rebels refused his authority, and in 1957 regional military commanders in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulawesi">Sulawesi</a> took over local government. He declared martial law in March 1957, nationalised 246 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> companies and expelled 40,000 Dutch citizens. Presidential Directive No. 10 of 1959 banned commercial activities by foreigners in rural areas; it targeted ethnic Chinese, and approximately 100,000 chose to return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. The PRRI-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/permesta">Permesta</a> rebels who formed in 1958 received money and weapons from the CIA, and were militarily defeated by the end of 1958.</p><p>Under Guided Democracy he disbanded parliament in 1960 and replaced it with one half of whose members he appointed, and with military backing disbanded the Masyumi party and Sjahrir's PSI. Opponents from Sjahrir to the Islamic politicians Mohammad Natsir and Hamka were imprisoned, and critical newspapers were closed. Vice President Hatta, who opposed guided democracy, resigned in December 1956. In 1963 the assembly elected Sukarno president for life, and a personality cult grew around him. Opponents attempted to kill him several times, including a grenade attack in 1957 in which six children were killed. His confrontation with Malaysia from 1964 and his withdrawal from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> in January 1965 were opposed by elements of the military. The economy was neglected: money printed to pay for military spending produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> above 600% a year in 1964 and 1965, and he was contemptuous of macroeconomics, unable and unwilling to offer practical solutions.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/suharto"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suharto.jpg" alt="Suharto" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/suharto">Suharto</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:President_Suharto,_1993.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">State Secretariat of the Republic of Indonesia, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By 1965 the PKI had three million members and Sukarno had approved the Communist Party chairman Aidit's call for a fifth armed force of peasants and workers. Before dawn on 1 October 1965 a group calling itself the 30 September Movement kidnapped and murdered six senior army generals. Major General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suharto">Suharto</a> took control of the army, and by 2 October the movement had failed. The army, together with the organisations <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammadiyah">Muhammadiyah</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahdlatul-ulama">Nahdlatul Ulama</a>, led a purge of communists and leftists, and the worst massacres were in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a>. The most widely accepted estimates are that at least half a million were killed, and the lede gives between 500,000 and over 1 million; as many as 1.5 million were imprisoned at one stage or another. Sukarno referred to the generals' killings as a ripple in the sea of the revolution and failed to protect the PKI.</p><p>Students demonstrated against him from January 1966. On 11 March 1966 he signed the Presidential Order known as Supersemar, which assigned Suharto to take all measures considered necessary for security and stability. Whether he was forced to sign, perhaps at gunpoint, is a point of historical debate. Suharto had the PKI banned and arrested officials loyal to Sukarno. Sukarno relinquished his executive powers on 20 February 1967, and on 12 March 1967 the MPRS stripped him of the title of president for life and the MPR named Suharto acting president. He was held under house arrest, where his health deteriorated for lack of adequate medical care, and died on 21 June 1970.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sukarno is called the Father of Proclamation (Bapak Proklamator). Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suharto">Suharto</a> his role in the independence struggle was minimised and his name largely removed from public discourse. As opposition to Suharto grew, and after his fall in 1998, public interest in Sukarno revived. His legacy as the founding father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> and a symbol of national unity and independence is widely respected by many Indonesians, often more so than Suharto's. The Soekarno-Hatta International Airport keeps the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> spelling of his name, which he insisted he wrote with a u. On 9 September 2024 the MPR revoked the 1967 resolution that had removed him from the presidency and barred him from politics, on the ground that there had been no trial to prove that he was responsible for the events of 30 September.</p><p>He is still fondly remembered for promoting the influence of newly independent countries, and that streets in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabat">Rabat</a> and a square in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a> bear his name. In the early 1960s his aggressive foreign policy raised tensions with Western powers, the economy declined and a personality cult grew. Some scholars have posited that Western fears of his plan for a rival to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>, CONEFO, were an overlooked factor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> support for Suharto's takeover. His daughter Megawati Sukarnoputri became the fifth president of Indonesia.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/indonesia-raya">Indonesia Raya<span>1928</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Sukarno (1901–1970) led the Indonesian nationalist movement, declared independence with Hatta in 1945 and was the country's first president. His Guided Democracy and campaigns abroad ended in 1965, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suharto">Suharto</a> took control and a mass purge killed at least half a million people.</em></p>

